riants that cause more severe  outcomes we need to be prepared to  adjust our approach as appropriate but  at the same time recognize that  individuals who are at higher risk will  need to take additional precautions for  themselves  those who are at lower risk again will  need to adjust based on their own risk  tolerance  and ultimately again i believe it is  absolutely critical that we work very  hard to  support a level of respectful dialogue  to help  ensure that the relationships in our  communities are strong because covet is  not the only significant issue that  we're facing together and  unfortunately over the last two years  there have been divisions that have  formed so i do think learning to live  with covet also means  learning to have those dialogues across  divisions  thanks dr hinshaw lauren follow up  i do thank you uh just an update dr  henshawn a question you were asked about  uh around the peak of the omicron wave  about the actual number of cases of the  virus since our pcr testing is so  limited and there are no plans to really  bring it back unless we need to with our  positivity rate hovering at around 20  how many cases do we likely have in the  province or how many are we catching  with testing  at that particular moment as you'll  recall i had estimated that we were  catching um  likely  around 1 in 10 was my estimate now  you'll recall that throughout most of  the pandemic based on our serology  results we estimate that we've caught  about one in four so it's not a new  reality that we've had more cases of  covet in the community than we've  diagnosed with our pcr program for many  reasons  often people are able to isolate at home  and they don't need to go in for testing  and that's fine so we were typically at  one and four moved to about one in six  for the delta wave based on our antibody  testing and and again i was estimating  in um in december that we were around  one in ten it  may have shifted to  be even more than 10 per individual case  diagnosed it's difficult to say  i haven't had a chance to look at the  the latest serology results in detail  but ultimately what i think is really  important for albertans to know is that  kovid is still out there and it is still  spreading so those who have risk factors  it's important for them to continue to  take precautions  at the same time the  prevalence the percent positivity has  come down that's about half of what it  was at the peak which is good news  continues to decline  so we will continue to watch that and  make sure that we are sharing  information that will help people to  make the risk-informed decisions  that  they're taking for their daily lives for  themselves and their loved ones  thanks dr hinshaw and minister copping  so that's it for today we'll see you  next week and of course between now and  then anyone with questions please feel  free to follow up with us separately so  thanks again
 

